The toolbox with costs, verified:

```js
// SEARCH — O(n) scans
arr.includes(x);        arr.indexOf(x);   // boolean / index
arr.find(p);  arr.findIndex(p);         // first match by predicate

// TRANSFORM — O(n), returns NEW arrays
arr.map(f);  arr.filter(p);  arr.reduce((acc, x) => acc + x, 0);

// COPY — O(n)
const copy = [...arr];  arr.slice(1, 3);

// MUTATE — in place (the trap list)
arr.sort((a,b) => a-b);  arr.splice(1, 2);  arr.push(x);  arr.reverse();
// non-mutating moderns: toSorted, toReversed, with, toSpliced

// STRINGS — immutable: every op returns a NEW string
s.split('').reverse().join('');   // 3 allocations
s.padStart(2, '0');  s.trim();  s.slice(-3);
```

The O(n²) string trap (THE classic): building with += in a loop copies the whole prefix each time — 1+2+...+n = O(n²). The fix: push pieces into an array and join once — O(n). Same class: repeatedly `str = str + ch` inside recursion.

The mutate-vs-copy axis (the #1 whiteboard mistake): sort/splice/push/reverse MUTATE — calling them on props or state you still need corrupts the source (React bugs live here: state arrays must be copied first, [...arr].sort()). The copy family: spread, slice, toSorted/toReversed (ES2023). In an interview, SAY which family you're using — 'I'll copy first because the caller still needs the original.'
